 Building a docs-authoring loop I can actually audit (11/2/26)

  LLMs write better docs every model release, but "better" isn't something I can ship on vibes. So I replaced a single-prompt "update the
  docs from this PR" skill with a loop: plan, judge the plan, approve, write, judge the doc, report. Each step is checked by something
  other than the model doing the work.

  The problem
  - One big skill prompt gave me no way to tell which instruction the model followed, skipped, or quietly ignored.
  - "It looks right" isn't a test. I needed pass/fail evidence I could trace back to what the agent actually did.
  - Style guides and skills keep growing, and newer models may not need half of them. I had no way to measure that.

  What I built
  - Task profiles: one loop with task profiles (restyle a page, update docs from an engine PR) instead of one skill per job.
  - Gates that can say no: hooks refuse a planner's or writer's handback until a contract check, deterministic validators (Vale, links,
    JSON blocks) and an LLM judge all pass, with a retry cap.
  - A run report built from Claude Code's own logs: every check row cites the exact log line it rests on and opens it at that line. Cost
    estimates match Claude Code's own totals to the cent on Opus. The report ships as an HTML artifact from GitHub Actions, and a hard $2
    budget hook stops runaway runs.
  - Tests that test the tests:
    - Fake loop sessions with deliberate faults: the writer skims a style guide, the planner edits a file, a gate retries past its cap.
      Each fault must trip exactly its own check.
    - Mutation testing: I break the code on purpose and confirm the tests notice.
  - Sync gates: CI fails if the spec, test catalog, tests, report checks and design docs drift apart.

  What I learned
  - Coverage overstates protection: the secret scrubber had 100% line coverage, and 15 of 29 deliberate breaks still passed, including
    switching off GitHub-token redaction.
  - Fault injection found real holes: a writer that read 5 lines of the glossary, or whose read failed, counted as "read the style guide".
    Both are now refused.
  - Validators found 4 real defects in a published page: dead anchors and a broken JSON example.
  - The judge rejected every plan for one page across 4 runs: the plans were mediocre, which turned "is the judge too strict?" into a
    calibration question I can label rather than argue about.

  What's next
  - Replay a real engine PR end to end and score it against the docs change a human actually shipped.
  - Ablation evals: drop each style guide and skill, one at a time, and see what newer models still need.
  - A weekly drift check, so Vale rules and the style guides stay in sync.
